## Portable Agent Baseline: Testing Practices

- A test whose expected result depends on an external mutable config it cannot itself set is not self-contained — two test cases needing opposite settings on the same shared target can never both pass in one run. Either own the setting (set it, then restore it, including on failure) within the test, or run against a fixture with the value already baked in.
- Before adding a test, ask what behavior it's actually verifying and where that behavior's existing coverage already lives — don't add a high-maintenance end-to-end test for behavior already stably covered by a unit test one layer down; add the expensive test only for what the cheaper layer genuinely cannot observe.
- Approval/E2E tests run against a shared remote environment reflect whatever build is currently deployed there, not your local code — before accepting a batch of failing snapshots as real behavior changes, confirm the deployed build actually includes your changes. Never approve new baselines generated by an environment that isn't yet running the new code.
- When requests all technically succeed but a whole batch of test assertions fails uniformly, suspect a shared test-harness assumption (e.g. a response-parsing path used by every test) before assuming the product has many independent bugs — use one passing peer test with a known-correct read path as contrast to localize the fault to the harness rather than the product.
- A new real dependency (a code path now resolving against a real DB/service instead of a mock) can pass every mocked test and still break a DB-backed integration suite — explicitly run every real-infrastructure suite for that path before declaring the change verified, don't stop at "all mocked tests pass."
- After a refactor moves *where* logic runs (not just renames it), re-check that a test targeting that logic still actually reaches it — grep the call site the test's setup depends on; a green suite doesn't prove the test still exercises the branch it was written to lock in.
- Before combining two independently-authored test builders/seeding steps that share a foreign key, check their defaults actually agree — don't assume they match just because both reference the same "TestData" constants; grep and compare each builder's own default.
- After adding a member to a widely-mocked interface, don't trust a green build — a loose/lenient test double silently defaults a new value-type member (bool/int/enum) with no exception, unlike an async member that typically throws unconfigured. Explicitly audit and configure every test double for the new member.

Apply this baseline before ordinary test-writing and test-triage habits, but never use it to override explicit user instructions, safety rules, privacy boundaries, or stricter repo-local instructions.
